# The serving configuration, in one file, because it is the thing that has to be reproducible.
#
# Everything here is a decision with a measurement behind it (docs/measurement.md); the ones
# that are not obvious:
#
#   LEN_FIXED=0    with LEN_LATCH=1: the drafter policy decides the block width once a REQUEST
#   LEN_LATCH=1    rather than once a block. Per-block routing loses to a fixed sixteen once
#                  arms start switching: switching arms costs acceptance whether or not the
#                  switch was a good idea. Four wide blocks, up to four narrow probes, one
#                  decision, then no more switching.
#   MAX_LEN        the KV buffer is allocated for all of it at load (tools/mem_audit.py):
#                  64 KiB a token of engine KV + 20 KiB a token for each of the two drafter
#                  arms (and 256 B of rotary table) = 104.25 KiB a token, 28.0 GB of KV at 256k, and 53.45 GB for the whole
#                  served stack in a fresh process.
#   CACHE_GB       the state cache's budget, after the KV has been paid for (OPS-47: 2 GiB, and
#                  the memory limits below).
#   FUSE_PROJ=0    the fused projection groups are lossless and worth ~1 %. They are OFF in
#                  the release candidate because the candidate is a stability release; set
#                  to 1 to measure them.
HOME_DIR=${HOME}
REPO=${HOME}/qwen38-spark-engine
PY=${HOME}/recipes/ling3-flash-dgx-spark/.venv/bin/python
PYTHONPATH=${HOME}/pylibs

HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8000
SERVED_MODEL=qwen38-spark-engine

# The published NVFP4 set (huggingface.co/0xBakeer/TandemLLM-Qwen3.8-27B-NVFP4), the one the StairCut paper measured.
NV=${HOME}/nvfp4-bf16/mlp.safetensors,${HOME}/nvfp4-bf16/gdn.safetensors,${HOME}/nvfp4-bf16/attn.safetensors
HEAD=${HOME}/nvfp4-bf16/head-fp8.safetensors
CKPT8=${REPO}/train/ft-b8-v2
CKPT16=${REPO}/train/ft-b16
CORPUS=${REPO}/corpus

MAX_LEN=262144
# Whole-file asks (level rows included) can exceed 16,384 tokens even inside a code block;
# the cap -- not the model -- was deciding the deliverable.
DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS=32768
# Anti-repetition. All empty/absent = the server defaults, which are the identities:
# penalties off, no pattern stop, byte-identical output. Per-request body params
# (`repetition_penalty`, `presence_penalty`, `frequency_penalty`) override the server defaults --
# Open WebUI model rows that already send `presence_penalty` are honoured the moment the engine
# restarts, so check the roster's values before flipping anything here.
#REP_PENALTY=1.05
#PRESENCE_PENALTY=1.0
#FREQUENCY_PENALTY=0.0
# A repeat-pattern guard (period:min_repeats:window). A tight floor (e.g. 64:1:8) can cut a
# legitimate generated file mid-line: a long run of identical tokens can be real data (a
# repeated separator or level row) rather than a degenerate loop, and no periodic-run detector
# can tell the two apart below "longer than a plausible row". OFF on the served engine: the
# thinking phase already has its own stall detectors, and an answer loop is bounded by
# max_tokens, whose cut is visible in the response. Set to e.g. 256:32:8 to re-arm.
PATTERN_STOP=
# The conductor's session monitor (server/conductor/monitor/, its policy ops/supervisor.yaml).
# raw: none of it runs, and nothing closes a reasoning block but a budget (the old stall close is
# the monitor's task D1 now). shadow: every task journals what it would do and nothing is done.
# on: each task acts in its policy mode. on since the :8000 service of 2026-10-01 (0.3.2-test), and
# the default of a new box from 0.3.21-test (the profiles' defaults are the live ones).
CONDUCTOR=on
#CONDUCTOR_POLICY=${REPO}/ops/supervisor.yaml
REASONING_FORMAT=tags
# low | medium | xhigh, or none / off: thinking off for a request that says nothing about it
REASONING_EFFORT=medium
LEN_FIXED=0
LEN_LATCH=1
BUDGET=16
# OPS-47 (the owner, 2026-10-01): the engine's memory limits. At load, empty, the engine holds 57 GiB
# at the 262,144 window; on 2026-10-01 it grew to 75 GiB live and 82.8 GB at night: the caches
# (14.5 GiB at their old caps), a long prefill's workspace (4.9 GiB at 172k tokens) and the
# allocator's reserve (7 GiB, no cap). The limits are in docs/server.md (OPS-47).
# Every limit below decides only where a prefill starts, never a token. The dashboard's Memory plan
# panel shows each one with what it holds now and may override it (the residency document): a
# key marked "restart" there applies when the engine restarts.
#   MEM_CEILING_GIB   the engine between requests, allocated plus reserve: 57 load + 10 caches +
#                     0.9 small + 2 reserve. A cache grows only while the engine stays under it less
#                     the slack; over it, caches go back cheapest first before the next prefill
#   MEM_PEAK_GIB      the ceiling plus a 262,144-token prefill's workspace (7.4 GiB); a prefill that
#                     would go over gives the caches back, then gets a 503 + Retry-After instead
#                     of a wedge. The residency planner subtracts it, not today's MemAvailable
#   RESERVE_SLACK_GIB free allocator blocks kept between requests; over it, empty_cache after the
#                     request has finished (never inside one; the graphs' pools are not touched)
#   RESIDENT_GB       the resident anchors: 17 of 147 MiB (the 4 tail lines, 8 head lines, 5 in the body)
#   RESIDENT_STASH_GB / RESIDENT_GUEST_MAX  a guest (opencode's title call, 1-3k tokens) up to
#                     8,192 tokens; 8,192 + 512 rows is 0.88 GiB
#   RESIDENT_PIN_GB   the pinned head: 11 pin hits reused 151,552 tokens (253 s) on 2026-10-01
#   RESIDENT_PARK_GB  one other session's rows past the shared head, swapped back on its next turn
#                     (A, B, A in opencode): about 35k tokens in 4 GiB
#   CACHE_GB          the state store: it serves chats under ~7k tokens (2 hits a night)
#   STORE_ENTRY_GB    one store entry; room is made before the clone, not after
# Caches: 2.5 + 1.0 + 4.0 + 4.0 + 2 + 0.5 (vision) = 14 GiB at their caps (+ 0.14 for the end anchor, 0.3.14), 4 more than the 10 the
# 70 GiB ceiling was sized on; the park gets what the ceiling leaves.
MEM_CEILING_GIB=70
MEM_PEAK_GIB=77
RESERVE_SLACK_GIB=2
RESIDENT_GB=2.5
RESIDENT_STASH_GB=1.0
RESIDENT_GUEST_MAX=8192
RESIDENT_PIN_GB=4.0
RESIDENT_PARK_GB=4
CACHE_GB=2
STORE_ENTRY_GB=1
# SPD-75: the block-8 drafter arm. 1 builds it (the served default); 0 runs the block-16 arm alone,
# about 8 GiB less (its 5 GiB context cache at 262,144 and ~3 GiB of weights and buffers), valid only
# with QWEN38_LEN_MODE=wide below. Off only after a same-hour speed comparison says no class is worse.
NARROW_ARM=1
# 0.3.14-test: the resident prefix's end anchor -- the recurrent state at the end of every
# generation (147 MiB, one), so the next turn that sends the answer back resumes there instead of
# re-reading it (33,172 tokens, ~44 s after a 32k thinking in rrr2); held to the session cache's
# greedy gate (docs/caches.md "After a long generation"). off = 0.3.13-test's resident prefix.
END_ANCHOR=on
# 0.3.21-test: the end anchor for a copy that differs from the rows in whitespace alone (a tool
# call's parameter value the parser trimmed, the template's newline before </think>): served with
# the rows' tokens up to the end anchor (server/endalign.py). off = 0.3.20-test's replay alone.
END_ALIGN=on
# Once the service answers, ops/start.sh drops the page cache of the weight files
# (tools/drop_page_cache.py, fadvise DONTNEED, no root): ~52 GB the engine never reads again,
# counted as available but not given back in time -- a large prefill can otherwise take
# MemFree close to 0 and trigger a GPU out-of-memory error. 0 keeps the cache (a restart then
# loads from RAM, not the NVMe).
DROP_PAGE_CACHE=1
# 3600: a whole-file rewrite needs 20-30k tokens at 15-20 tok/s, and a 900 s wall clock cuts
# them mid-file. A runaway is still capped; the queue is single-sequence anyway.
REQUEST_TIMEOUT=3600
MAX_QUEUE=8
# 240: while a long agent run holds the engine, a second request can wait rather than be
# refused outright. 240 s stays under Open WebUI's ~300 s OpenAI client timeout, so a request
# that can be served waits instead of erroring. Beyond that the answer is concurrency, not
# patience.
QUEUE_TIMEOUT=240
FUSE_PROJ=0

# Set explicitly so the serving configuration is readable in one place even where it agrees with
# the code default. Both were tried; run-to-run noise on this board (~10 % on the mean between
# two runs of the same engine) makes them hard to resolve against each other, so they are at the
# values the release candidate was soaked on, and they go to the `speed` branch to be measured
# against several rows each.
# `nodes`: the served tree budgets never leave room for a branch until the narrow arm's budget
# grows past its block; `nodes` then accepts 8-17 % more than `paths` (tools/tree_sweep.py).
QWEN38_DF2_TREE_MODE=nodes
QWEN38_TREE_ALIAS_STATE=0

# Release the arm that loses the latch: no tap, no sync, and nothing of it in the state
# snapshot. Lossless (tools/verify_spec.py --drop-idle reports `identical` on every workload),
# and on the paired five-workload bench it is ahead of the router on 15 of 15 comparisons --
# five workloads times three repeats -- for a median of +3.2 % and a mean of +4.0 %, with
# accepted-per-block equal or better everywhere. The saving is largest exactly where the
# engine is fastest, because what stops is a `sync` over the ACCEPTED rows: +1.5 % on prose at
# 2.7 accepted a block, +7.1 % on edit at 12.6.
DROP_IDLE=1
# How sharply the DRAFTER's proposal distribution is tempered relative to the request.
# Sampled-with-drafter measured ~30 tok/s at 1.0 (the draft's spread over wrong tokens) and
# 35-43 at 0.3-0.5 on the same prompt; 0.3 is the served default and the request body may
# override with draft_temperature.
QWEN38_DRAFT_TEMP=0.3
# A sampled request verifies the greedy request's TREE, the target's token drawn at each node
# and the walk following the child that carries it (`det`), instead of the q-aware chain.
# Exact by construction: the tree never depends on the draws it is walked with. Sampled
# five-workload bench (temperature 0.7, draft 0.3, 3 rounds alternated): tokens a round
# 4.07/4.15/4.08 -> 5.21/5.33/5.27 (+28 %), tok/s 44.6/45.0/44.7 -> 53.1/54.1/54.1 (+19..+21 %),
# every workload resolved better. On the board the answers match the target sampled alone
# (tools/sampled_dist.py: 2,400 + 1,920 answers, min p 0.023 / 0.056 against a Bonferroni
# 0.0011 / 0.0033, the 0.5-temperature control seen at 1e-7 / 2e-11). Greedy is not touched
# (the flag is read only for a sampled request): identity and lossless gate PASS, greedy
# answers identical, nothing resolved worse. `mixed` (the sampled spine) is exact too and
# slower than det on every workload. Unset = the q-aware chain.
SAMPLED_TREE=det
# rc3: the fused GDN prefill kernel. Gate: tools/prefill_quality.py, 8k/16k, PASS (NLL delta
# <= 0.005 nats; divergence no worse than the control). TTFT 760 -> 691 ms on the row; decode
# neutral (fixed-policy A/B overlaps).
QWEN38_FUSED_GDNPREFILL=1
# rc4 (speed2), three flags adopted together on rows taken with the persistent suffix store
# OFF (every earlier row otherwise read its own answers back from it). Against two store-off
# baselines, two candidate rows each: mean +5.8..+7.9 % RESOLVED, TTFT 690-709 -> 427 ms
# RESOLVED, nothing resolved worse; clean-store view mean +4.8 % RESOLVED. Lossless gate PASS
# with all three.
#   QWEN38_DECODE_ATTN   decode/verify attention reading the KV once (tools/attn_kernels.py):
#                        decode at a 32k prompt 7.43 -> 16.46 tok/s; quality gate = the control.
#   QWEN38_DRAFT_NVFP4   the block drafter's projections in NVFP4: draft call ~24 -> ~14 ms.
#   QWEN38_NVFP4_PREFILL_V2  the v2 W4A16 tile for 33..1023-row prefills: TTFT -38 %; quality PASS.
QWEN38_DECODE_ATTN=1
QWEN38_DRAFT_NVFP4=1
QWEN38_NVFP4_PREFILL_V2=1
# phase4 (not deployed): the v2 tile for the 1,024-row chunks too. The prefix cache forwards
# every prompt in 1,024-row chunks and the old bound (1024, exclusive) sent each full chunk to
# the unpack path, where gate/up is 24 % slower than v2 and down level (tools/prefill_crossover.py;
# unpack wins from 2,048 / 1,536 rows). Served-path TTFT 8k 12.1 -> 10.6 s, 16k 24.4 -> 21.5,
# 32k 30.5 -> 26.7 (-12 %); prefill_quality at 2k/8k/16k, chunk 1,024: NLL -0.0009 / +0.0002 /
# +0.0014 (per prompt <= +0.0043), divergence not worse than the control -- PASS; quality-gated,
# not bit-gated (a prefill has no ulp identity).
QWEN38_NVFP4_PREFILL_V2_UNTIL=1025
# rc4 (kernels), seven flags adopted together on the new-text row. Against rc4's speed2
# defaults (cand-nostore / -r2 / cand-clean), the bundle (kb-nostore, kb-nostore-r2, kb-clean):
# mean +21.9..+25.0 % RESOLVED, p50 +23.9..+29.1 % RESOLVED, p90, max, TTFT and wall resolved
# better in all six comparisons, nothing resolved worse; 33.9-34.7 mean / 31.2-32.1 p50.
# Lossless gate (verify_spec, the served tree router and the chain router, five workloads):
# PASS. In-engine block 123 -> ~99 ms.
#   QWEN38_NVFP4_SKINNY     W4A16 for 1..32 rows in CUDA, a packed byte straight into a tensor-core
#                           operand (tools/nvfp4_skinny.py); gate/up 193 -> 220 GB/s in the engine
#   QWEN38_SKINNY_TILES     its per-shape tile table, the one the rows ran (nt2:wk16:pf2)
#   QWEN38_FUSED_COMMIT     the block's commit for all 48 GDN layers in one kernel, no state copies
#   QWEN38_FUSED_GDNVERIFY  a verify block's GDN mixer in four kernels a layer
#   QWEN38_DRAFT_FC_NVFP4   the drafter's context projection in NVFP4 (drafter only)
#   QWEN38_FUSED_ADDNORM    residual add fused into the next RMS norm (bit-identical)
#   QWEN38_TREE_HOST_DEPTH  a tree verify's depth from the host, not 48 device reads (bit-identical)
#   QWEN38_HOST_WALK        the drafter's greedy walk with one device read (same tokens)
QWEN38_NVFP4_SKINNY=1
QWEN38_SKINNY_TILES=${REPO}/ops/skinny-tiles.json
QWEN38_FUSED_COMMIT=1
QWEN38_FUSED_GDNVERIFY=1
QWEN38_DRAFT_FC_NVFP4=1
QWEN38_FUSED_ADDNORM=1
QWEN38_TREE_HOST_DEPTH=1
QWEN38_HOST_WALK=1
# phase1, adopted one item at a time on branch `phase1` on the rows' block time: each item's
# ms/blk resolved better against the adopted set before it in every pair (store off twice,
# clean once), tokens/block not resolved worse, nothing resolved worse (ops/gate.sh).
#   QWEN38_VERIFY_GRAPH     the verify from CUDA graphs; ms/blk 99.1/98.9/98.6 -> 95.8/95.8/95.5
#                           (-3.2..-3.4 %), tok/blk 3.33/3.30/3.26 (not resolved), mean +2.4..+3.4 %
QWEN38_VERIFY_GRAPH=1
#   QWEN38_COMMIT_IN_VERIFY the commit folded into the next verify's recurrence; ms/blk on top of
#                           the graph 95.8/95.8/95.5 -> 93.7/93.9/93.4 (-2.0..-2.2 %), tok/blk not
#                           moved; with the graph, mean +5.7..+6.7 % over rc4k, resolved in all pairs
QWEN38_COMMIT_IN_VERIFY=1
#   QWEN38_DRAFT_GRAPH      the draft call from a graph per context class (512/1,024/2,048); ms/blk
#                           on top of graph + fold 93.7/93.4 -> 92.7/92.7/92.6 (-0.9..-1.0 %), tok/blk
#                           not moved; the set's mean +6.6..+8.4 % over rc4k, resolved in all pairs
QWEN38_DRAFT_GRAPH=1
# phase2, adopted on branch `phase2` by the ratified per-item rule (ms/blk resolved better
# in every comparison, nothing resolved worse; a p90 regression the same configuration does not
# reproduce on a re-run is noise) -- ops/gate.sh.
#   QWEN38_GDNV_ONE_WARP    one warp a GDN verify recurrence program; ms/blk 92.7/92.7/92.6 ->
#                           91.5/91.5/91.4 (-1.2..-1.5 % against the deployed rows and the re-run),
#                           mean/p50/tok_blk unchanged; p90 resolved worse in 1 of 3 rows (one prompt's
#                           text, mix-0248, flips on a near-tie), not reproduced on the re-run
QWEN38_GDNV_ONE_WARP=1
#   QWEN38_TREE_NODES_NARROW  the narrow arm's tree at 16 nodes from its 7-slot lattice -- the
#                           same 16-row verify the served wide arm pays -- built node-wise (its
#                           QWEN38_DF2_TREE_MODE=nodes above), priced on the measured verify curve
#                           (QWEN38_TREE_MS, verify + commit at 8/16/24/32 rows).
#                           Gate eng107c against the set before it and the deployed rows: tok/blk
#                           3.30/3.26 -> 3.64/3.71/3.76 (+10..+15 %), ms/blk +0.4..+2.2 %, mean
#                           36.9/36.4 -> 39.9/40.3/40.4 (+8..+11 %), p50 +11..+21 %, nothing resolved
#                           worse (max 85.6-85.7, TTFT 420-424 ms). Lossless PASS.
QWEN38_TREE_NODES_NARROW=16
QWEN38_TREE_MS=8:77.9,16:78.9,24:87.0,32:97.0
#   QWEN38_TREE_NODES + QWEN38_TREE_WIDE_AFTER  the wide arm's tree at 24 nodes, but only once the
#                           request has committed 32 tokens (a short answer never pays the 24-row verify
#                           for branches it cannot use: the row's max is a 41-token answer), on the
#                           verify curve past 16 rows (QWEN38_VERIFY_ROWS=32 + the 17..32-row tile table
#                           that keeps each shape's K split). Gate eng107d against the deployed rows: mean
#                           36.7/36.3 -> 41.5/42.0/41.4 (+13..+14 %), p50 +17..+18 %, tok/blk 3.32/3.28
#                           -> 4.02/4.04/4.02 (+21..+23 %), ms/blk +4.7..+5.8 %, max and TTFT not
#                           resolved; against eng107c (narrow 16 only): mean +2.5..+5.1 %, tok/blk
#                           +7..+11 %, max -2.6..-3.2 % in the two store-off rows (the 41-token request's
#                           last block). Lossless PASS with 32-node trees.
QWEN38_VERIFY_ROWS=32
QWEN38_SKINNY_TILES_WIDE=${REPO}/ops/skinny-tiles-wide.json
QWEN38_TREE_NODES=24
QWEN38_TREE_WIDE_AFTER=32
#   QWEN38_DEEP + QWEN38_DEEP_AFTER  after two wide blocks in a row commit their whole width,
#                           the lookup drafter's exact continuation of the request's own text as one
#                           chain of up to 32 rows. A serving default: lossless (verify_spec at 256
#                           tokens, three deep blocks on edit: identical), row-neutral by construction
#                           (spd12 41.08/41.88/41.45 against eng107d 41.53/41.95/41.40, nothing
#                           resolved either way), edit +15 % on the five-workload bench (15.75
#                           accepted a block against 12.60).
QWEN38_DEEP=32
QWEN38_DEEP_AFTER=2
#   QWEN38_LEN_SWITCH + QWEN38_LEN_MODE=wide  the node count of every round calculated on the verify
#                  staircase: the wide drafter only, each candidate cut to the count that maximises its
#                  expected committed tokens per ms, the narrow drafter released at the first round.
#                  QWEN38_STAIR_TABLES prices the cut by context length (chain and tree apart);
#                  QWEN38_STAIR_RHO gives the lookup's continuation rate on 8-token matches;
#                  QWEN38_STAIR_SKIP prices the head-skip rule on the same staircase. The
#                  tables were measured on the NVFP4 set from the BF16 release.
QWEN38_LEN_SWITCH=1
QWEN38_LEN_MODE=wide
QWEN38_STAIR_TABLES=${REPO}/ops/stair-tables-nvfp4.json
QWEN38_STAIR_RHO=${REPO}/ops/lookup-rho.json
QWEN38_STAIR_SKIP=1
# phase5: the skinny kernel reads its e4m3 scales from a copy laid out in runs of 16 rows x one
# 128-wide K step (128 contiguous bytes), made on a weight's first skinny call. A permutation of the same bytes read in
# the same order: bit-identical (kernel battery, 162 outputs; identity against the release tree, every tensor).
# Alternated A/B, store off: ms/blk 94.88 -> 92.50 (-2.5 %), mean +2.9 %, both resolved, nothing resolved worse. The
# set (phase5 + this) against the release alternated in one hold: store off mean 42.38 -> 43.90 (+3.6 %), ms/blk
# 95.29 -> 92.74; clean 42.69 -> 43.91 (+2.8 %), ms/blk 94.66 -> 92.00; nothing resolved worse. Costs +1.45 GiB (the
# copies of the 400 target projections' scales).
QWEN38_SKINNY_SRUN=1
# phase6: the e4m3 head GEMM at BN 32, 4 warps, 6 stages (bit-identical to 64:4:3, +2 % GB/s)
# QWEN38_HEAD_GEMM=32:4:6  # OFF: OutOfResources (102400 > 101376 B smem) on a penalty request with an 11.7k prompt (head GEMM over many rows); default 64:4:3 until the config is sized per M

# The usage ledger -- one SQLite row per request (counts and times, no text), 400 days, backed
# up weekly beside it. ops/start.sh passes it to the :8000 service only; the server never takes
# it from the environment, so row3 / gate / soak servers do not write it.
QSE_USAGE_LEDGER=${HOME}/.qwen38-spark-engine/usage/ledger.sqlite3

# The conductor (server/conductor/): the one model `TandemLLM` (SERVED_MODEL above becomes a hidden
# alias of it; raw is the `X-Tandem: raw` header), its versioned config (profiles, task router,
# roles, packages) and its decision journal, edited from the dashboard's Conductor view.
# ops/start.sh passes it to the :8000 service only, as the ledger.
# Empty or off: no conductor, /v1/models lists the raw engine alone.
QSE_CONDUCTOR_CONFIG=${HOME}/.qwen38-spark-engine/conductor
# SRV-441: the router's fit to the served model (server/conductor/modelfit.py, docs/server.md "The model fit"):
# model (the adapter's; LFM2.5 declares one, the Qwen models none), off (the router as written), or key=value,...
#QWEN38_CONDUCTOR_FIT=model

# The proxy (proxy/, docs/proxy.md): other backends beside the engine (vLLM, SGLang, any
# OpenAI-compatible server, OpenAI, DeepSeek), routed by model id, with the Backends screen in the
# dashboard. The path of its config database; empty or off: no proxy, the service as before.
# ops/start.sh passes it to the :8000 service only, as the ledger. TANDEM_* variables here overlay
# the database (the environment wins); keys are better kept in the database (encrypted) or in
# secrets.env.
QSE_BACKENDS=

# Parallel requests (server/slots.py, docs/parallel-requests.md): QSE_PARALLEL lanes, slot 0 and N - 1 side
# slots over one copy of the weights; two or more decoding requests verify their trees in one pass (up to
# 16 a pass; more lanes verify in more passes). 1 is one sequence. SRV-707: 8 lanes, every lane the same
# window, MAX_LEN (QSE_SIDE_MAX_LEN 0; a shorter value is kept for old setups). A side slot is built only
# while MemAvailable stays at or above QSE_PARALLEL_FLOOR_GB with its bytes added, and a request waits
# while its worst case would push MemAvailable under it.
QSE_PARALLEL=8
QSE_SIDE_MAX_LEN=0
QSE_PARALLEL_FLOOR_GB=15
# A side slot's own verify graphs (0.3.44-test): on holds ~7.5 GiB of graph pools a slot; off (the
# default with 8 lanes) verifies eager when the slot decodes alone (a joint pass is eager either way), so
# a slot costs ~1.5 GiB beyond its KV. QSE_SIDE_SLOT_CONST_GB is the build's estimate of that constant.
# docs/parallel-requests.md "Beside the small models" has a box's memory plan with both on.
QSE_SIDE_GRAPHS=off
QSE_SIDE_SLOT_CONST_GB=1.5
# The shared KV pool (SRV-307, engine/kvpool.py, docs/parallel-requests.md "The shared KV pool"): on, every
# lane's window is reserved as addresses, and its KV pages (64 KiB a token of engine KV, 2 x 20 KiB of
# drafter KV) are committed only as a conversation's rows are written and given back when it ends or under
# memory pressure. Bit-identical to the static buffers. QSE_KV_HARD_FLOOR_GB: a commit never takes
# MemAvailable under it (the caches, then idle conversations are given back first). off: every window
# allocated at load, so 8 lanes of 262,144 tokens would not fit; a profile whose engine has no verified pool
# sets it off and its lanes' windows are memory at load.
QSE_KV_POOL=on
QSE_KV_HARD_FLOOR_GB=10
# SRV-311: with side slots, a round's waiters time out while a slot that is not decoding (a prefill) holds
# the GPU turn, so chats keep decoding beside a long prefill (3 chats 4.5 -> 10.7-14.3 tok/s beside an 87k
# prefill, which itself takes ~18 % longer: the decoders get their share). off = the old barrier.
QSE_BARRIER_IDLE_OWNER=on
# The small models under memory pressure (server/rolemem.py): when the KV pool needs pages, the caches go first,
# then the shadow reader, then the narrator, and only then an idle conversation is parked or a request waits.
# They load again through the residency planner once the headroom (MemAvailable less cache growth and the
# requests' reservations) has stayed at QSE_PARALLEL_FLOOR_GB + the role's claim + QSE_ROLE_RELOAD_MARGIN_GB
# for QSE_ROLE_RELOAD_S with nothing waiting, one at a time; a role unloaded again within QSE_ROLE_THRASH_S of
# its reload waits twice as long (up to QSE_ROLE_MAX_HOLD_S). Laya stays loaded unless QSE_ROLE_UNLOAD_LAYA=on
# (it decides routing; its fallback is Qwen's own answer, which can change what the model is given).
# QSE_ROLES_DYNAMIC=off: the roles stay loaded as before.
#QSE_ROLES_DYNAMIC=on
#QSE_ROLE_UNLOAD_LAYA=off
#QSE_ROLE_RELOAD_MARGIN_GB=4
#QSE_ROLE_RELOAD_S=60
#QSE_ROLE_THRASH_S=600
#QSE_ROLE_MAX_HOLD_S=3600

# phase3, adopted on branch `phase3` by the per-item rule (ops/gate.sh --mode block) against
# the deployed Phase 2 rows (p2final).
#   QWEN38_GDNV_CONV_BLOCK / _WARPS  the GDN verify convolution at 32 channels a program on
#                           one warp (320 programs, not 40 walking the block's rows): bit-identical (flags on ==
#                           off on the real model), -0.7..-1.5 ms a 48-layer block on the bench; the rows' ms/blk
#                           98.26/98.37 -> 97.32/97.39 store off (resolved in all four pairs), clean 97.42 ->
#                           96.38 on the re-run pair (resolved; against p2final-clean's 96.98 not resolved), nothing
#                           resolved worse, lossless PASS.
QWEN38_GDNV_CONV_BLOCK=32
QWEN38_GDNV_CONV_WARPS=1
#   QWEN38_GDNV_WY (+ _WY_FUSED, the MAXT thresholds)  a verify block of up to 16 rows
#                           (the narrow arm's 16-node trees, the wide arm's first chains) takes the GDN recurrence
#                           in the WY form -- every row at once, (I + A) d = beta (v - exp(G) K S0) -- with the
#                           convolution and the gates inside its two kernels; longer blocks walk (the fused prep
#                           is pathological at a 32-row tile). Same mathematics in another order (per-row updates
#                           1e-7 of float64), lossless PASS; gate spd38 on top of step 1: ms/blk 97.32/97.32/96.38
#                           -> 95.94/96.54/95.69 (-1.4/-0.8/-0.7 %, resolved in all three), nothing resolved worse.
QWEN38_GDNV_WY=1
QWEN38_GDNV_WY_FUSED=1
QWEN38_GDNV_WY_MAXT=16
QWEN38_GDNV_WY_CHAIN_MAXT=16
QWEN38_GDNV_WY_FUSED_MAXT=16

# The live activity on /v1/dashboard/live. LIVE_ACTIVITY=off is the kill switch (the 1.0 view,
# new fields null); QSE_LIVE_HZ is its busy cadence (1, 2, 4; default 4).
# The live rates leave each request's warm-up out: QSE_LIVE_DECODE_WARMUP_TOKENS (default 50),
# QSE_LIVE_PREFILL_WARMUP_CHUNKS (1), over QSE_LIVE_NOW_WINDOW_S (2); a prompt with under
# QSE_LIVE_MOSTLY_CACHED_PCT (10) % or under QSE_LIVE_MOSTLY_CACHED_TOKENS (4096) new tokens is
# "mostly cached". Unset = the defaults; timings, usage, the ledger and /metrics never use them.
LIVE_ACTIVITY=on

# The dashboard login. It matters only with an admin password (QSE_ADMIN_TOKEN in secrets.env)
# set: then on makes /v1/dashboard/* need the token or a session too. With no password set (the
# default) every admin route is open and the page asks for nothing; docs/server.md "The admin rule".
QSE_DASHBOARD_LOGIN=off

# OPS-41: the roles beside the engine, loaded by ops/roles.sh after /health, one at a time through
# the residency planner (claims, floor and order: the conductor's `residency` document). None
# today; a role is ROLES_AUTOLOAD="decider" plus ROLE_DECIDER_START / _HEALTH / _RUNTIME / _WAIT.
ROLES_AUTOLOAD=

# ENG-179 / SRV-131: the decision models this engine serves itself (engine/decision): Laya's
# checkpoints laya-english, laya-multilingual, laya-typed-decisions (the HF repo
# convaiinnovations/laya under QWEN38_DECIDER_DIR; pull it with the HF token). Empty: off, and
# nothing of engine/decision is imported. With names: /v1/systemone answers on this port behind the
# admin token, the conductor's decider role (endpoint self) asks in process, and start.sh asks the
# engine for the `deciders` document's on_start models after the roles, one at a time through the
# residency planner. Questions run only while no request holds the engine (stage A).
QWEN38_DECIDER_MODELS=
QWEN38_DECIDER_DIR=${HOME}/.qwen38-spark-engine/deciders/laya

# P1 (2026-10-02): the narrator, a small generative model inside the engine (engine/generative) that
# writes the state lines for a profile with `ui.narrator: on` (server/conductor/narrator.py), and N2
# (2026-10-03) a summary of each stretch of thinking with `ui.narrator: thoughts`: qwen3.5-0.8b or
# qwen3.5-2b (Qwen/Qwen3.5-*, BF16, the engine's own forward, the 27B's tokenizer; pull them with the
# HF token), or minicpm5-2b (P1's, the lines only). QWEN38_NARRATOR_DIR empty: the directory
# ~/.qwen38-spark-engine/models/<name>. Empty model: off, and nothing of engine/generative is
# imported. v3 (2026-10-04, `ui.narrator: live`, the voice's live lines; server/conductor/voice.py):
# ops/start.sh loads it at the start through the residency planner (role `narrator`, 6 GiB) after
# the decision models, and the server's keeper loads it again in an idle window whenever it is not
# loaded.
QWEN38_NARRATOR_MODEL=
QWEN38_NARRATOR_DIR=

# SR1 (2026-10-04): the shadow reader (engine/shadow.py, server/conductor/shadow.py, docs/shadow-reader.md):
# a Qwen3.5 small model (qwen3.5-0.8b, qwen3.5-2b or qwen3.5-4b, the 27B's token ids) keeps its own
# state of each session of a profile with `shadow.mode: on`, read in the idle window, exact on its
# grid. Empty: off, nothing imported. It shares the narrator's slot when both name the same model,
# else it loads its own through the residency planner (role `shadow`: the model's claim plus
# QWEN38_SHADOW_GB). QWEN38_SHADOW_GB: resident session states at most (past it the least recently
# used goes to QWEN38_SHADOW_DISK, `auto` = ~/.qwen38-spark-engine/cache/shadow, at most
# QWEN38_SHADOW_DISK_GB, or is dropped); QWEN38_SHADOW_GRID: the rows of one read (the exactness grid).
QWEN38_SHADOW_MODEL=
QWEN38_SHADOW_DIR=
QWEN38_SHADOW_GB=4
QWEN38_SHADOW_GRID=512
QWEN38_SHADOW_DISK=auto
QWEN38_SHADOW_DISK_GB=20

# ENG-181 (2026-10-02): the probe snapshots (server/probe.py). on: a TandemLLM request with tools
# gets Qwen's rows copied at the router's three decision points (the last message's last token,
# every QWEN38_PROBE_EVERY reasoning tokens, a finish candidate: the 5 tapped layers and the
# post-norm row, 61,440 bytes a point, no extra forward) into the ring under QWEN38_PROBE_DIR,
# 30 days, at most QWEN38_PROBE_DAY_MB a day; raw and chats without tools never. Off by default.
QWEN38_PROBE_SNAPSHOTS=off
QWEN38_PROBE_DIR=${HOME}/.qwen38-spark-engine/probe

# C1 (2026-10-02, 0.3.13-test): the disk tier (engine/disk_cache.py) -- prompt-prefix state on the
# NVMe under the caches in RAM: a prefix prefilled twice, and the first long request of an opencode
# session (its ~24-30k-token head), written behind the request and read back where it starts later
# than everything in RAM (bit-identical to a cold prefill on the grid). A directory, or off. Its
# budget (50 GB), TTL (7 days), admission (2) and on/off are the conductor's settings document
# (memory.disk_cache), edited on the Memory screen's Disk cache tab; it never takes the NVMe under
# 50 GB free. ops/start.sh passes it to the :8000 service only, like the ledger. On for the :8000
# service since 0.3.15-test, and the default of a new box from 0.3.21-test.
QSE_DISK_CACHE=${HOME}/.qwen38-spark-engine/cache/disk
# rc-0.8.0: the tier knows its files by content (fingerprint fp4), and its first start adopts what
# 0.7.2 and the RC's earlier builds wrote for the same rows. A tree moved to another folder names the
# folder it came from here, once (colon-separated), so 0.7.2's files, which carry their folder, are
# adopted too: QSE_DISK_ADOPT_FROM=${HOME}/tandem-live (docs/caches.md "A tree in another folder")

# 0.3.22-test (engine/spill.py): a live conversation on the NVMe, under the disk tier's directory
# (sessions/): written whole at a planned stop (SIGTERM's drain) and read back by the next start
# before its first request; written before a request of another session that is not a guest
# replaces a resident conversation the RAM park cannot hold (16,384 tokens and up), and read back
# by its next turn. Its own budget (64 GB from 0.4.3-test, was 40), the tier's 50 GB free-space
# floor, 48 h unused. off = 0.3.21-test. Needs QSE_DISK_CACHE.
SESSION_SPILL=on
# 0.4.3-test (SRV-271/273/275): the spill's budget (64 GB: three big chats compressed; the head
# cache's 16 GB is the settings document's memory.disk_cache), new blocks compressed losslessly
# (zstd over byte planes, 1.71x on a real entry; off = written as they are), and the day's NVMe
# write budget past which optional writes stop (the tier's, the spill's small and parked ones)
SESSION_SPILL_GB=64
SESSION_SPILL_CODEC=zstd
DISK_WRITE_BUDGET_GB=200
# The vision tower (the checkpoint's own, 0.86 GiB, and the 512 MB cache of encoded images; off: text
# only) and the response cache (an identical greedy request answered from memory, 256 MB; opt-in, never
# on a server a benchmark measures). ops/start.sh passes them; unset is on and off.
VISION=on
RESPONSE_CACHE=off
# OPS-65: the status responder that answers for the engine while it is away (docs/operations.md);
# empty: off. The reference box uses 8009 (its reverse proxy tunnels it beside :8000); install.sh sets
# the API port + 9 (OPS-175), so the Setup Guide's restart screen has it. It listens on QSE_STATUS_HOST,
# else HOST
QSE_STATUS_PORT=

# Flash-Next (OPS-99): a flashnext-<bpw> profile (ops/profiles/_flashnext.env) sets these; the 27B reads
# none of them. QSE_ENGINE_FLAGS takes the place of the 27B's model and drafter flags in ops/start.sh.
# The table: warm keeps the n-gram table in the page cache (every floor check counts it, SRV-335, and the
# page-cache drop after a start leaves it, QSE_PAGE_CACHE_KEEP); cold reads it past the page cache
# through a row cache of FN_TABLE_CACHE_GB (ENG-239). The rest are the engine's defaults
# (engine/settings.py), listed so they are found here.
#QSE_ENGINE_FLAGS="--model ${HOME}/flashnext-packs/3.05 --drafter mtp"
#QWEN38_FN_TABLE=warm
#QWEN38_FN_TABLE_CACHE_GB=2
#QWEN38_FN_DRAFT_HOT=32768
#QWEN38_FN_LOOKUP=1
#QWEN38_FN_PREFILL_CHUNK=4096
#QSE_PAGE_CACHE_DROP=${HOME}/flashnext-packs/3.05
#QSE_PAGE_CACHE_KEEP=ngram_embedding.safetensors

# Weight profile (ops/use-profile.sh): ops/active-profile names ops/profiles/<name>.env, which sets only the
# weight-specific values (weights, drafters, draft head, sampler nucleus, disk-tier directory) over the ones
# above. QSE_PROFILE_OVERRIDE reads another profile without switching (use-profile.sh's checks). No file:
# the values above, as before. A community pack's profile, community/<id>, is the file ops/use-profile.sh
# writes from its registry entry under run/ (server/setup/community.py; QSE_COMMUNITY_ENV_DIR: a dry
# run's own folder instead). A missing file must not fail the assignment: bin/tandem and the setup apply job's
# checks source this file under set -e (OPS-159).
QSE_PROFILE_NAME=${QSE_PROFILE_OVERRIDE:-$(cat "${REPO}/ops/active-profile" 2>/dev/null || true)}
if [ -n "${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}" ] && [ -f "${REPO}/ops/profiles/${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}.env" ]; then
    . "${REPO}/ops/profiles/${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}.env"
elif [ "${QSE_PROFILE_NAME#community/}" != "${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}" ] && \
        [ -f "${QSE_COMMUNITY_ENV_DIR:-${REPO}/run}/${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}.env" ]; then
    . "${QSE_COMMUNITY_ENV_DIR:-${REPO}/run}/${QSE_PROFILE_NAME}.env"
fi

# Community packs (docs/community-weights.md): the setup guide fetches their index from this URL when its
# weights step opens and on Refresh, never on a timer; unset is the public repository's main, off fetches
# nothing (the last fetched copy, else the release's own, is used)
#QSE_COMMUNITY_URL=off

# The setup guide's overlay (docs/setup.md): the keys the dashboard's #/setup wrote with its last
# apply (server/setup/apply.py). Read after the profile and never a weight key, so the profile still
# decides the weights; this file stays as it is, and reset level 2 removes the overlay. No file: the
# values above, as before. QSE_SETUP_ENV reads another overlay without writing it (the plan's preview).
QSE_SETUP_ENV_FILE=${QSE_SETUP_ENV:-${REPO}/run/setup.env}
if [ -f "${QSE_SETUP_ENV_FILE}" ]; then
    . "${QSE_SETUP_ENV_FILE}"
fi
